.         gen same_hh = (m_same_hh==1 | f_same_hh==1)

.         drop m_same_hh f_same_hh

. 
.         
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H2 *
. 
.         * Verbal description of our model: Event-study regression with year F
> E
. 
.         * Hypothesis 2. Having daughters leads to more left-leaning political
>  preferences
. 
.         * Accordingly, we run a event-study regression with the leaning inten
> sity variable as dependent variables. The event is the birth of a daughter as
>  a first child compared to a son. We control for survey wave (syear), the emp
> loyment status (empl), years of education (educ), years of education squared 
> (educ_sq), migration background (migback) and sample region (sampreg), sex (s
> ex), whether a child is still living in the same household (same_hh) and numb
> er of kids (no_kids) as independent variables
.         
.         * the identifying assumption is that conditional on giving birth, the
>  gender of the child is exogenous. We believe this assumption is fulfilled, s
> ince it is illegal to tell the parents about the sex of an embryo before abor
> tion can no longer be performed without risking legal punishment in Germany. 
> Furthermore, there is some evidence that female embryos seem to have a higher
>  chance of survival in stressful pregnancies (e.g. Hamoudi & Nobles, 2014) bu
> t this does not seem to meaningfully impact sex ratios at birth in Germany. T
> herefore, we believe the identifying assumption to be fulfilled.
. 
. 
.         xtreg intensity_leaning $event_time $event_daughter_time i.syear i.em
> pl educ educ_sq i.migback i.sampreg i.sex i.no_daughter i.no_son, fe vce(robu
> st)
